	"nodes":[
		{"id":"ae834cd96fb5c776","type":"text","text":"## Ancillary Services","x":-420,"y":-252,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"8a6ac187b079e234","type":"text","text":"# A new view on system strength - Watt Clarity - 4/06\n\nArgues that GFL is not inherently bad, just different, and that a low inertia grid actually has many advantages when compared to a high inertia grid. We should focus instead on better transmission capability and optimising GFL to GFM ratio rather than imposing penalties on solar and wind projects for using GFL inverters. \n\nhttps://wattclarity.com.au/articles/2026/06/reframing-the-grid-discussion-from-system-strength-to-system-behaviour/?_gl=1*1uizbah*_ga*MTUyMDYyOTAyMy4xNzgwNTI2NzEy*_ga_4ZTT9PF6TR*czE3ODA1MjY3MTIkbzEkZzAkdDE3ODA1MjY3MTIkajYwJGwwJGgw","x":-125,"y":-110,"width":386,"height":100,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"e825a77c1514c066","type":"text","text":"# Transmission Shortfall in India - Ember - 5/06\n\nTransmission backlog caused by supply gap for HVDC, fragmented land ownership, right of way disputes, and biodiversity clearances. This, along with concentrated solar and wind, caused 300GWh of solar curtailment in Q1 alone. \n\nReactionary planning of transmission lines to generation buildout. You can fix this by adding stronger regulatory support for BESS resource pooling that creating a government backed body that can aggregate projects to contract to BESS developers, and allow BESS to be procured as a grid asset to be socialised across transmission charges.","x":71,"y":-376,"width":381,"height":74},
		{"id":"f7cb0231681c42fc","type":"text","text":"# ~={orange}Electricity Market=~","x":-85,"y":-930,"width":301,"height":72,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"74648b2066572d59","type":"text","text":"## NEM","x":-525,"y":-800,"width":250,"height":50,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"8cf11538bf913076","type":"text","text":"## News\n","x":-1072,"y":-671,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"3ed94f554e0243ad","type":"text","text":"# Grid Planning","x":-633,"y":-474,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"414f6481b022479f","type":"text","text":"# Addressing stochastic variability in forecasting\n\nGrid forecasting in the NEM and ESOO is done on stochastic data but plans for a deterministic outcome, which is the central scenario. However this does not address the worst case scenario, which is what we should be planning for. \n\n- The article calls for a reframing of how we can better plan for a grid that is becoming more variable and weather dependent.\n- When you plan for the centre, you strip the headroom required for the worst.\n\nThe article calls for us to move beyond POE10 and POE50, drawing on much larger weather datasets.\n- Bring gas demand and transport limits into analysis, too often are they left at the edge of the model when they are the system's reserves\n- Add wargaming, stress testing, and red team/blue team exercise into the grid\n\n\n","x":-1001,"y":-279,"width":359,"height":87,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"d699e9c47f97fa2b","type":"text","text":"# Grid Operations","x":-840,"y":-990,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"924f6a21f79e3c7b","type":"text","text":"# Performance report for events above $5000/MWh\n\nConsiders the market trends, event or behavior surrounding competition and efficient market operation, where the wholesale market price >$5000/MWh for the 30 minute period.\n- These events drove uo the volume-weighted average price by $7/MWh\n\nThere were 3 separate events in Q4 2025, compared to 7 the previous quarter and 23 the same period last year.\n\n- In all three instance, high prices were driven by low solar and high demand due to high temperatures during the summer\n- One event also contributed to rebidding behavior, where participants withdrew their low price and moved capacity from low to high prices for technical or commercial reason\n\nThe reduction of grid scale solar must be replaced by higher priced generation and reduction of rooftop solar also means that demand needs to be met from the grid.\n\n## Units being unable to ramp up \n\nAround 1700 MW of low priced capacity was unable to be dispatched as units were not able to ramp up quick enough to respond to the pricing event.\n\n- The ramp rates of 9 units across 4 market participants had their ramp rates at a minimum setting. The AEMC has submitted a rule change where generators have to submit a ramp rate that reflect the technical/maximum capacity of the plant, rather than using it to achieve commercial objectives.\n\nAnother constraint was caused by planned network outage in interconnectors that prevented cheap bids from entering the market.\n","x":-1180,"y":-1200,"width":343,"height":120,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"b5b2f677ee18d637","type":"text","text":"# Thermodynamics\n","x":1053,"y":-1013,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"8b5748dab6df1aa1","type":"text","text":"## Exergy - Online course\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b94HAcQXQY4&list=PLsegmWko7aP_U8oGH5IzRXepXjx5_06k6&index=2\n\nThe work potential of energy, or available energy.\n- When a system is in thermodynamic equilibrium with the environment, then none of that energy system can be used to produce work. This is known as the dead state.\n\t- For example, there is no pressure or temperature gradient\n\nThe total energy can be split into \n- Energy with zero exergy content \n- Exergy of the system\n\nTherefore exergy is the energy that takes you above the equilibrium. And a system delivers the maximum possible work as it undergoes a reversible process from the specified initial state to the dead state, the state of the environment.\n\nKinetic energy and potential energy are $100$% exergy.\n\n## Reversibility and Irreversibility\n\nThe more irreversibility, the less efficient the system. \n- Remember that more entropy = more irreversibility because that's energy which can be work\n\n$W_{rev}$ is the maximum amount of work that can be produced.\n\nWhile the Surroundings work is the work done by or against the surroundings during a process. For example, when pushing down on a cylinder in a constant process,$$W_{surr}=P_0(V_2-V_1)$$\n$W_{useful}=W-W_{surr}$\n\nWhere useful work is the same as work is in steady flow devices, rigid tanks, and cyclic devices, as no work is being done to the surrounding to expand them.\n\nUsing this definition, irreversibility is the difference of reversible and useful work. $$I=W_{rev,out}-W_{useful,out}$$or it can also be defined for the input process $$I=W_{in}-W_{rev,out}$$\nTherefore, irreversibility can be viewed as the lost potential to do work\n\n\n","x":975,"y":-858,"width":406,"height":78,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"210a79e9eb9f6bec","type":"text","text":"## Batteries and smart EV and heat pumps are ready to scale in Europe 24.06.2026\n\nhttps://ember-energy.org/latest-insights/batteries-and-demand-flexibility-are-ready-to-scale-across-the-eu/\n\nBy 2030, 1 in 5 EU households could have a heat pump and 15% of heat pump demands could be shifted.\n\n- Batteries are also to become cheaper than gas peakers by 2030. Also driven by a global shortage in gas equipment\n- Estimated at 20% lower cost than gas peakers (for batteries). This estimate is by the ACER\n\nAccelerateEU has an indicative storage target of 200GW. How did they come up with this number?\n- Utility scale batteries is set to 10-fold and home batteries will double (key barrier being high upfront costs)\n\nCurrent projections highlights that heat pump installations could exceed 40 millio by 2030 (RePowerEU has a target of 60 million). With supportive demand flexibility policies, EU peak demand can be shaved by 3% using 15% of heat pump capacities.\n\nDemand flexibility is dependent on smart meter rollouts and consumer incentives.\n\nIn January 2027, all EU countries are expected to release their indicative national objectives for storage and demand flexibility.\n\nLook to how Bulgaria, Italy, and Spain demonstrate how policies can accelerate deployment.\n- Avoid grid double charging\n- Enable participation in multiple markets to de-risk revenue streams","x":270,"y":-611,"width":420,"height":120},
		{"id":"9e2d3ed4d1f43c57","type":"text","text":"# Podcast interview with SMA inverter CEO - 06.26\n\nHosted by renew economy's podcast on Spotify\n\nThe podcast touched on the growth of SMA from the 1980s, and how it grew into $2 billion in revenue by the 2000s. However chinese comepetition reduced revenue to $800 million within 1-2 years, where they went through restructuring\n\nSMA inverters now have their revenue stream from 80% utility and 20% domestic\n\n- There are a lot of government regulation influencing inverter purchases at the utility scale. Due to data security concerns. This is as inverter manufacturers can issue updates to the inverter, and a lot of stuff is software based now.\n\nFurthermore, the CEO talked about how GFM inverters can fully balance the grid. \n- Many community batteries now means that price spike events are getting more and more infrequent.\n- VPPs are needed to regulate the price spike events. However, small scale home batteries are regulating market efficiently\n\n\n\n","x":-1740,"y":-871,"width":426,"height":121,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"3b925ed5eab9a65e","type":"text","text":"# Changes to VicGrid Access laws 6.07.2026\n\nhttps://reneweconomy.com.au/grid-access-rules-to-be-changed-but-wont-penalise-advanced-projects-that-are-ready-to-build/\n\nVicGrid is currently moving away from the NEM wide access regime from July 2027, replacing it with a system that prioritises development within REZ. This is to ensure that projects within the REZ are guaranteed to not be impacted by projects outside of it.\n\n- Draft still expected, but it is on the basis that open access creates challenges for developers in choosing where to invest\n\nThose outside the REZ have to undergo the alternate grid impact assessment pathways\n\nHowever, for projects that are well advanced and have been built around the current open access regime, (those with grid connection letter, state planning approval, or CIS contract), there will be a one off process to secure access rights.","x":-696,"y":207,"width":376,"height":114,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"2e4a859bc2e4b15e","type":"text","text":"# 2026 CEIG Survey 08.07.2026\n\nhttps://reneweconomy.com.au/they-are-losing-patience-just-one-in-ten-investors-believe-australia-will-meet-renewables-target/\n\nPeople who rate Australia as an attractive place to invest in renewable energy is down from 69% to 58%. \n- With the EPBC reforms in 2026, $4$ projects managed to reach FID at the end of last year\n\nThe main challenges according to stakeholders in renewable energy investment are,\n- Transmission buildout delats\n- Planning approvals\n- grid connection process and negative pricing\n- Access to grid and congestion\n- Lack of cleat market incentives (PPA liquidity and contract tenor)\n- EPA\n- Coal closure certainties\n- Community opposition\n- Tax treatment\n- Lack of incentives and funding\n- Supply chain/workforce constraints\n- Energy policy uncertainties\n- Lack of coordination\n\nThere are hopes that data center demand will kick off the transmission buildout required for more renewable energy. (Which is funny because data centres are a rebound effect rom cheap renewables)\n\nTo accelerate these projects, investors believe that accelerated transmission, revenue certainty, deepened offtake market is required.\n- Open projects up to competing bids \n- Make TSOs accountable for on-time delivery\n","x":-1072,"y":468,"width":405,"height":92,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"6def6446c4a2e46a","type":"text","text":"# ~={green}Fault Events=~","x":-1119,"y":-830,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"819df4c1245018c5","type":"text","text":"# South Australian wind volatility on 20.06.26 to 23.06.26\n\nhttps://wattclarity.com.au/articles/2026/06/still-air-empty-tanks-and-a-hot-joint/?_gl=1*1hqqpv1*_ga*OTg3MDYyNzA2LjE3ODI3MzQwMzE.*_ga_4ZTT9PF6TR*czE3ODI5MDQ5MTkkbzIkZzAkdDE3ODI5MDQ5MTkkajYwJGwwJGgw\n\n![[Screenshot 2026-07-01 at 9.25.06 pm.png]]\n\nWind fell away on the 20th to 23rd of June 2026. , leading to a lift in gas fired generation. However, there was no price volatility in Victoria. Notably, there was actually net negative transfer away from SA to VIC during price spike.\n\nBatteries entered Monday with almost nothing in the tank. Price popped during this event and on monday morning when there was no recharging window over the night.\n- This may be due to autobidders for BESS, where thresholds above $300/MWh led to BESS pulling from the market. Note that autobidders take spot price forecasts, but the demand shown may not have been volatile enough for the batteries to invest in this risk\n\n## The interconnector story\n\nConstraints on a 220kV line downstream of the interconnectors PEC-1 and VSA leads to dispatch reducing or even reversing the flow on the interconnector.\n- The dispatch solution was proving to be very expensive, as electricity was being dispatched from SA where prices were reaching $15,000/MWh to relieve the X3 constraint\n\nTherefore the ~={red}lack of levers=~ led to the inter-connectors being pulled. utlimately, with the battery on Monday being dead, there was not enough generation in SA to reverse the flow.\n\n- Ultimately, this meant that X3 was taken out of service with a breaker. As the inability to reverse flow will overload the line\n\nIt was found that the derating was caused by a hot joint issue, which is when connection to that piece of equipment, located at the end of the transmission line, loses conductivity and starts to heat up when high current passes through it.\n","x":-2000,"y":-611,"width":344,"height":101,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"d602014597b26ba5","type":"text","text":"# Australian Energy usage and the Kardashev scale - Renew Economy- 4/06\n\nArticle talks about Australian renewable curtailment and how it had reached 7,200 GWh in 2025, doubling figure in 2024. Would be interesting to look more into the breakdown of this.\n\nhttps://reneweconomy.com.au/the-kardashev-scale-australias-staggering-energy-transition-and-its-untapped-opportunity/","x":-2386,"y":-359,"width":386,"height":169,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"1dec40a9aa655e7f","type":"text","text":"## Tariffs on modified steel projects will impact renewable costs in Australia - RenewEconomy 6/06\n\nRetrospective tariffs of 48% on hollow steel tubing from China, Taiwan, Malaysia, and South Korea. The material is used for making the torque tubes that support the mounting of solar modules.\n\nAustralian manufacturers are arguing that the items are being sold below cost price and interferes with domestic ability to stay competitive.\n\nThis could affect GWs of solar projects starting construction. Exact cost will vary, but the decision can take months to resolve and lead to delays for planned, anticipated, and under construction projects. ","x":-1930,"y":-352,"width":381,"height":100,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"d83605c145bc7c71","type":"text","text":"# Operational Forecasts - AEMO / WattClarity\n\nhttps://wattclarity.com.au/articles/2026/05/an-introduction-to-operational-forecasting-at-aemo/\n\nThe Operational Forecast Team is responsible for providing operational forecast capabilities. \n\nThe produce the Forecast Uncertainty Measure (FUM) which is used to determine the lack of reserve triggers.\n\n- They make models and tools to help predict constraints and dispatch in the neat term.\n- Also support connect asset by evaluating Energy Conversion Models in the NER3.7B, check SCADA signal consistency. They also onboard non scheduled registration applications to see if forecasting is required. (Participants can also submit their own forecasts and the OPF assist with this)\n- They also keep an eye on the weather conditions for potential disruptions to VRE","x":-1487,"y":-343,"width":346,"height":83,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"bf5517aacc74491c","type":"text","text":"# Shifting to renewables due to supply chain risks - 26.06.2026\n\nInconsistency in fossil fuel prices caused by the war on Iran. \n- 200% swings in energy supply is unsustainable and encourages people to switch to independent fuel source\n\nLook to Fortescue in the Pilbara region\n- >1 GW of solar \n- Hundreds of MW of wind, \n- GW of battery storage\n- Hundreds of kms of transmission infrastructure\nThis will lift the renewable share in supplying energy to its Pilbara mine operations from 2% to 25%. \n\nAdopted self-lifting wind turbines and modular battery storage, and automatic solar pilings to help deploy renewable technologies at scale.","x":-1312,"y":-196,"width":342,"height":123,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"66f564d0b0fd8f8a","type":"text","text":"# ~={green}Heat Market=~","x":0,"y":-1260,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"8dace9e1e8ea6f1e","type":"text","text":"# Industrial District Heating in Denmark for the Processing industry 03.2026\n\nhttps://hotcool.dbdh.dk/no-3-2026-d-h-connecting-demand-and-supply/4/#zoom=true\n\nProcess heating accounts for half of the heat demand in Europe as a whole. The industry is looking for new synergies with District Heating, and there is currently no assessment of the potential. \n\nThe INTREPIDH project looks at 12 case studies of sector coupling between process heat and district heating. The key learnings from this project are \n- A lot of research going into high temperature heat pumps\n- The food and beverage industry is well suited to use DH to replace natural gas\n- Strong match for hot water processes ($60-110\\degree$C)\n\t- Food and beverage, paper pulp manufacturing, agri-food, low temp chemicals, and pharmaceuticals\n- There are already strong examples where this sector coupling is occurring.\n\nIt's a win-win-win scenario. Factories can reduce operating cost and carbon emissions and district heating system can achieve higher levels of utilisation.\n\nSome key considerations to look at when considering these issues:\n- heat demand profile\n- business model\n- access to electricity\n- distance to grid\n- temperature needed\n- steam required?\n- Available land footprint\n- energy efficiency\n- investment cost\n- heat price and its fluctuations\n- carbon emissions\n- reliability\n\n## Question\n\nWhat are the modelling approaches currently in place to optimise heat loss and heat transfer in a district heating system. A key issue is low temperatures is needed to reduce energy losses and increase the COP factor of heat pumps. On the other hand, high grid temperatures are needed to reduce the process temperature lift and improve EE of high temperature heat pumps.\n## Further Readings\n\nZagreb - brewery based in a dense urban region that uses district heating to supply high pressure steam at $260\\degree$C.\n\nLook at the case studies in INTEPIDH!\n","x":-345,"y":-1820,"width":423,"height":175,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"deec3acd2e0d58c1","type":"text","text":"## Calculating the irreversibility of a heat engine\n\n![[Screenshot 2026-06-24 095707.png]]\n\nRemember that irreversibility is the maximum amount of reversible work that is translated into useful work. In this case, the maximum amount of reversible work is the calculated using the Carnot efficiency. Note that this defines the exergy of the system, we cannot get anymore work out of the energy we put in because we cannot go below the ambient temperature.\n\n$$\\begin{align}\nW_{rev,out} &=\\eta_{th,rev}\\dot{Q_{in}} \\\\\n&=(1-\\frac{T_{sink}}{T_{source}})\\dot{Q_{in}} \\\\\n&= 0.75\\times 500kW \\\\\n\\therefore I &= 375-180=195kW \\text{ is the irreversibility}\n\\end{align}$$\n\n## Second law efficiency\nThis allows us to find the second ;aw efficiency, we describes the percentage of maximum reversible work we got out of the system\n\n$$\\eta_{II}=\\frac{180}{375}\\times 100\\%=48\\%$$\n\nThis is a way of measuring what's the best that we can do to reach our maximum efficiency. How close am I to the best case scenario. For example when you think about how fast you can run, you don't compare yourself to Usain Bolt.\n\n- Heat engines: $\\eta_{II}=\\frac{\\eta_{th}}{\\eta_{th,rev}}$\n- Work producing devices: $\\eta_{II}=\\frac{W_{u}}{W_{rev}}$\n- Work consuming devices: $\\eta_{II}=\\frac{W_{rev}}{W_u}$\n- Heat pumps: $\\eta_{II}=\\frac{COP}{COP_{rev}}$\n\n## Exergy efficiency\n\nThe second law efficiency is \n\n$$\\eta_{II}=\\frac{X_{recovered}}{X_{expended}}$$\nNote that $X_{recovered}$ is the work output, and $X_{expended}$ is the work output plus any exergy destroyed.\n\n![[Screenshot 2026-06-24 102008.png]]\n\nIn this case, $100\\%$ efficiency means that the COP is $1$, and one unit of electricity is required to make $1$ unit of heat. However, the maximum COP in a reversible environment is $$COP_{HP,rev}=\\frac{1}{1-\\frac{T_L}{T_H}}=26.7$$\nTherefore, the second law efficiency is $$\\eta_{II}=\\frac{1}{26.7}=3.7\\%$$","x":975,"y":-710,"width":402,"height":99,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"ecd6c6e8bb63def4","type":"text","text":"# Exergy of a compressed air tank \n\n![[Screenshot 2026-07-15 at 9.09.21 am.png]]\n\n","x":980,"y":-524,"width":405,"height":181,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"d191fdedb456f39e","type":"text","text":"# Exergy of a geothermal liquid","x":1423,"y":-509,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"ea836e8a406243d3","type":"text","text":"## Semi dispatch cap causes downwards frequency drift - 7/06\n\nhttps://wattclarity.com/market-reform/general-reform-initiatives/semischeduled/sustainable-or-scalable/\n\nSemi-scheduled generators are required to operate in two distinct modes, \n\n- Semi Dispatch cap OFF - Generator can generate to its natural resource limit\n- Dispatch cap ON - due to constraints in the transmission or being dispatched down due to increasing incidence of negative price in the dispatch (priced out). The unit must take account of the Dispatch Target when it operates.\n\nFor a fully scheduled generator, the target is the number the generator must generate, while for semi-scheduled plant, the target is a cap.\n\nSDI- the semi dispatch intervals is a 5 minute forecast. This SDC causes downward deviation in generator availability.\n\nThis will cause a down drift in frequency, so a larger volume of raise regulation FCAS whenever SDC is on.\n- VRE units with SDC on and availabiltiy above cap is being inhibited from delivering PFR because this would take their output above cap and they would become non-conforming.\n- We are wasting the capability of those resources with additional capabilities to resist downward frequency drift","x":360,"y":29,"width":360,"height":100,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"a02f71b80ff8997a","type":"text","text":"# Capacity Investment Scheme\n\nUnderwriting agreements where projects are subsidised by government if thy fall below a floor and if they exceed revenue ceiling, benefits are shared with the government. \n\n- It acts as an incentive for an additional 36GW of capacity by 2030 (22GW of renewables and 14GW of dispatchable)\n\nThese tenders are hosted by auctions. Benefits to reliability, emission reduction, and price reduction is weighed against project risks as assessment of the project. ","x":-799,"y":680,"width":383,"height":147,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"ed8b354b1b5c5dcb","type":"text","text":"# Is the battery fleet beginning to compete with itself? 14.07.2026\nhttps://wattclarity.com.au/articles/2026/07/is-the-nems-battery-fleet-starting-to-compete-with-itself/?_gl=1*tje28v*_ga*MTUyMDYyOTAyMy4xNzgwNTI2NzEy*_ga_4ZTT9PF6TR*czE3ODM5ODk1MzAkbzkkZzAkdDE3ODM5ODk1MzAkajYwJGwwJGgw\n\nLooked at two metrics\n- Price spread: The average of two highest two hours of 5 minute spread and the average of two cheapest hours. Reflects ideal hours of arbitrage\n- Percentage of perfect: percentage of revenue gained with perfect foresight, ceiling calculated with linear programming.\n\nFalling two hour price spreads in all four regions. More offered capacity, smaller spread. \n\nHowever, there is also a rising floor effect where the cheapest two hours cost money to charge on average.\n\nSoft peak can be caused by cheaper gas, milder weather, more wind. The rising floor price is due to higher demand during the middle of day that soaks up the midday sun.\n- Decades of accumulation of solar asset has pushed the midday floor down, and it is now coming back up with more storage assets.\n\nWillingness to pay of the battery fleet in all states are going up to above $0$. \n- The data show that there is a strong correlation between the bidding price and the clear price of these fleets, showing that the batteries are the marginal price setters\n\nHowever, with battery growth, there is also increasing gas, decreasing coal availability, and climate that can contribute to similar effects. \n- Thus we can expect alternative commercial arrangements for batteries as the case for day to day arbitrage erodes. The peak pricing events are still there, but that comes form chance days no one can build a business plan around.\n\nMeasure price correlations against nameplate, and there is a clear negative relationship to price. Measure it to offered energy within the fleet and the price fall is less steep. \n- This suggests that battery operators are adjusting","x":-360,"y":486,"width":339,"height":102,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"ade109cbd714468f","type":"text","text":"## FCAS - Watt Clarity 6/06\nhttps://wattclarity.com.au/articles/2026/06/06june-regulation-fcas-trend/?_gl=1*ujguto*_ga*MTUyMDYyOTAyMy4xNzgwNTI2NzEy*_ga_4ZTT9PF6TR*czE3ODA5NjA0NzUkbzIkZzAkdDE3ODA5NjA0NzUkajYwJGwwJGgw\n\nThere are 10 types of FCAS\n- 2x Regulation (Raise and Lower)\n- 6x Contingency (Raise and Lower)\n- 2x Fast Frequency Response \n\nArticle provides good sources for reading on the Australian FCAS market \n\nThe question for the FCAS market in the future is\n1. The rise of battery storage and the deployment of auto-bidders\n2. The need to ramp up the amount of Regulation FCAS enabled to confront the increasingly visible limitations of the Semi-Scheduled Category\n3. Increasing conflicts between Regulation FCAS Services and the provision of Primary Frequency Regulation (That differs in how they're triggered, response speed, and compensation structure). Note that PFR are inherent in scheduled and semi-scheduled generators and are traditionally unpaided. There are automatic responses to local frequency changes that acts in milliseconds, enabled by inertia. Comparatively, Regulation FCAS is dispatched centrally by AEMO via AGC that occurs every 4 seconds to bring the frequency back tot he 50Hz target. The ancillary service is traded on a spot market.\n\nFCAS prices can have periods of sustained volatility in island and potential islanding events.(where generator continues to supply power even though the grid is isolated from the main network)","x":-125,"y":97,"width":381,"height":151,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"1f20c93413989ad3","type":"text","text":"# Frequency Performance Payments (24/03/25) \n\nImplemented last year, it's a replacement to the causer's pay process for the allocation of Regulation FCAS.\n\nA good summary of the FCAS market in Australia.\n\n![[Screenshot 2026-06-11 081239.png]]\n\nEssentially FPP changes such that PFR is able to receive money from the PFR money pot for helping alleviate frequency deviations.\n- There's some indication that this PFR pot of money will be larger than the regulation FCAS pot of money.\n- This is as the FPP provides both an incentive for PFR and penalty for contributing to frequency fluctuations. This reduces the growing requirement for the increasing volume of regulation FCAS.\n\nUnder the causers pay methodology, we saw generators completely avoiding the FCAS cost recovery process, as we see when units obtain a 0 MPF (market Participant Factor) (That means that all generators will pay for causing frequency deviations)\n\n![[Screenshot 2026-06-11 082757.png]]\n\nThere is also however, a penalty and incentive scheme built into this mechanism, (0 sum gain), where the penalty occurred will equal to the incentive payments. \n\n![[Screenshot 2026-06-11 083209.png]]\n\n- The primary beneficiary are the batteries and coal, who are the price-setters in the market. This may cause FCAS regulation price inflation.\n- This mechanism might slow the growth in requirements for Regulation FCAS, helping to constrain Market Power and keep prices low.\n\nhttps://www.etsi.energy/\nLook at this link to understand the dynamics down to the individual generator","x":330,"y":173,"width":360,"height":145,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"979899e42e8150e9","type":"text","text":"# Review of Frequency Performance Payments - 44 days in\n\nhttps://wattclarity.com.au/articles/2025/07/frequency-performance-payments-44-days-in/\n\nHighlights potential for the FPP recovery costs to be higher than the regulation recovery cost.\n\nThe FPP adds in a default contribution factor to generators, where regulation FCAS that is used are recovered in proportion to unit performance in the interval through a negative contribution factor. While the cost of regulation FCAS that is not used are recovered in proportion each unit's historical performance, a default contribution factor. So even if a generator did well one day, there will still be a default payment, as opposed to the causer's pay mechanism previously implemented\n\nThe first 44 day data shows that the recovery cost is greater than the payments given.","x":840,"y":176,"width":340,"height":145,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"cc3a877b7155caf8","type":"text","text":"# Impacts of FPP on solar farms\n\nhttps://reneweconomy.com.au/frequency-penalties-are-costing-some-solar-farms-millions-but-this-is-the-market-working/\n\nSolar penalties are paying for $32$% of all frequency performance payments between June last year and April this year, despite only producing $8.7$% of total generation on the east coast.\n\n- This is mainly due to variability in weather that causes sudden changes outside of their control\n- The big winners have been batteries, followed by coal and hydro.\n\t- ~={orange}Why are they the big winners?=~ \nTotal frequency-related costs average at $0.38/MWh over the initial June-April period, approximately $1.23$% of wholesale market revenue. \n\nHowever, you can be in the same region and still be on both sides of the coin. Wrong forecasts can penalize you heavily, and therefore the operating strategy of the SF can affect FPP cost recovery heavily.\n\nSFs are increasingly operating at night to take advantage of their batteries. But although they aren't generating power, they are still charged as a grid participant for ancillary and frequency control services.\n- Furthermore, they are also charged extra in the middle of the day, during the solar peak when they aren't really making any money.\n","x":840,"y":514,"width":422,"height":144,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"5f9e5d0e0c538eec","type":"text","text":"# One year of FPP -17.06.2026\n\nhttps://wattclarity.com.au/articles/2026/06/fpp-anniversary-solarfarms/\n\nNote that the FPP is a net-zero sum game where the penalties for the unhelpful participants are paid to the helpful participants.\n- Rather than the causer pays framework, FCAS are allocated more directly to each participants' contribution to frequency performance\n\nNote that the the FCAS regulation costs and the FPP rewards and penalties are influence not only by the unit's frequency deviation, but also \n- prevailing system conditions\n- overall frequency performance\n- value of frequency regulation services at the time\n\n![[Screenshot 2026-06-25 113954.png]]\n\nNote that batteries are controllable and have reduced rates of penalties and increased opportunity for rewards when stabilising frequencies.\n\nAssets have raise regulation services that can either be used or unused, but will still count in frequency costs in their portfolio.\n\nAn important factor is purely by chance, caused by sudden weather variations that significantly affect the asset's FPP liability. Article highlights strong relationship between high cost events and rapidly changing weather, increasing the asset's exposure to frequency related costs.\n- Solar farms are also being charged at night, as unused FCAS regulation costs are allocated using broader cost recovery mechanisms that apply to market participants whether or not they are generating.\n- Risks are also generated through operational decisions, 12% of solar revenue is earned in just 0.2% of intervals. With co-located battery, operational decisions increasingly need to account for not just the next dispatch window, but market conditions over the coming hours and days.\n\t- The article examined solar farms under cloud cover and saw variance in FPP payments due to operational strategies being different across the region\n","x":452,"y":457,"width":280,"height":80,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"bc2900dd2de98321","type":"text","text":"# ~={red}FCAS Tutorial Series=~\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UP4YjgDhTzU","x":137,"y":598,"width":403,"height":60,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"2891622da32e2dae","type":"text","text":"# GenCost 2025-26 Renew Economy Reporting 15.07.2026\nhttps://reneweconomy.com.au/solar-and-wind-remain-backbone-of-least-cost-future-grid-as-batteries-squeeze-gas-to-a-fraction-of-the-mix/\n\nCSIRO published the new 2025-2026 GenCost report which shows that solar and battery is still the cheapest cost way to achieve net-zero.\n\n- There has been falling spot market prices from 2022 ($189/MWh) to 2025 ($104/MWh)\n\nThe war in Iran has not really fluctuated spot market prices because of the lower reliance on gas. However, these global pressures means that the technology will not be getting cheaper anytime soon, except solar PV and batteries, driven by China's unstoppable momentum in technological advancement.\n\nGas is not fast enough to supply the demand for data centres.\n\nElectricity prices must rise due to the replacement of old infrastructure. \n- Coal and gas will be $107-180/MWh$. While for solar PV and wind, the cost is $152/MWh $with transmission costs. Excluding transmission costs, this is $120-130/MWh$\n\nThere will be a limited cost of gas, contributing $3-7\\%$ of the total generation. \n\n","x":-1649,"y":288,"width":449,"height":124,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"fbb83e0d370e2c29","type":"text","text":"# It needs to be more than big batteries - AEMO Connection Scorecards 20.07.2026\n\nhttps://reneweconomy.com.au/its-got-to-be-more-than-big-batteries-the-triumphs-and-failures-of-australias-green-energy-transition/\n\nReview of the AEMO connection scorecards.\n- Main fact is that there needs to be more wind and solar capacity built to meet the 2030 target of 82% renewables.\n- In the 2025-2026 FY, 9.1 GW of new generation and storage capacity reached full output, which is more than double that of the previous year\n- But more than 5GW of that is in the form of big battery capacity\n- Therefore, less than 4GW of new wind and solar is less then what is needed for the 2030\n\n## What is the trend\n\nInvestment in wind has slowed down in recent years due to rising cost, planning issues, transmission capacity, and social license.\n\nAEMO received 12 wind projects applications this year, totaling 5.2 GW.\n\n![[Screenshot 2026-07-21 103445.png]]\n\nHowever, registration and application approvals were at (7.4GW and 14.2GW last year, dominated by big batteries)\n\nConnection process were once the key contributors to project delays, but it is now project changes. Design changes and equipment substitutions are causing this.\n- There are also not a lot of firm buyers, utilities are okay without renewables as that means their coal plants will get paid out \n- Hope is that data centers will provide some buying power'\n\nFurthermore, the median duration of project implementation has increased from 14 to 18 months. \n\n- There have been 4.3GW of solar and battery hybrid applications, and 2.4 GW of solar projects have started the process to add a battery","x":-1423,"y":724,"width":383,"height":156,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"bbc7719d5d686be2","type":"text","text":"# The ABC of Electrified District Heating - Large Scale Heat Pumps 01.2026\n\nhttps://hotcool.dbdh.dk/no-1-2026-power-up-district-heating/16/\n\nelectricity based heat generation offers stronger coupling between power and electricity sectors. The load of a district heating system can be divided into \n\n- base, year round load\n- mid, seasonal load\n- peak load during the coldest days of the year\n\nElectric heat pumps are expensive to install and cheap to operate (therefore need a high utilisation rate). Therefore, it is rarely feasible or cost effective to meet the entire demand of the district heating system with heat pumps alone.\n- Other alternatives might be an e-boiler\n\nThus, there will be a shift of district heating from net producers to net consumers. How will this affect the electricity grid\n## Further readings\n\nLook at Sdr Felding, which has a 2.5MW het pump, a 10MW electric boiler, and 3000 $m^2$ of storage. Although max demand is only 5MW. They make a lot of money offering services to the electricity grid.\n- Billund district heating system\n","x":125,"y":-1520,"width":402,"height":120,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"6dfa8d78bb019714","type":"text","text":"# Electrification, Flexibility, and Thermal Storage - How the Billund District Heating reduce heat prices by 25% through ancillary services\n\nhttps://hotcool.dbdh.dk/no-1-2026-power-up-district-heating/4/\n\n","x":279,"y":-1820,"width":411,"height":175},
		{"id":"be0b76f82ada8567","type":"text","text":"# Hibernation traps and administrative lag for high velocity resources - Renew Economy 13.05.26\n\nhttps://wattclarity.com.au/articles/2026/05/the-velocity-of-flexibility-avoiding-the-iprr-hibernation-trap/\n\nThere is currently a lag between when VPPs and Voluntary Scheduled Resources (VSPs) are technically available and when they are legally allowed to bid. \n\nThis comes from the status notice obligated by the Integrating Price Responsive Resources (IPRR) framework, where VSR and VPPs have to update their administrative status after long downtimes (around long scheduled maintenance or Christmas)\n\nThese services may be sidelined due to administrative lag when it returns from hibernation.\n- E.g. when your resource comes back on early, you may face penalties for a deviation that your software can handle, but administrative status does not permit\n\n![[Screenshot 2026-06-22 145241.png]]\n\nIn the case of a hibernation (downtime > 14 days), VIM incentive payments are paused and a formal status change must be lodged with AEMO.\n\nThese lags makes resources unable to respond to price spike events and artificially inflates the WACC for VPP projects, as investor sees that regulatory friction can sideline their asset, they will demand higher returns to compensate for that risk.\n\nTo fix this, the article author suggest the use of a telemetry validated reserve to replace manual notice periods.\n- Also implement compliance buffers to protect providers and encourage them to return to market sooner.\n- Have dynamic VIM re-activation such that the VIM incentive eligibility is restored the moment the plant becomes active.","x":-824,"y":-73,"width":424,"height":140,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"962814a8e64b0135","type":"text","text":"# Operation of a heat pump\n\nhttps://www.engproguides.com/pressure-enthalpy-diagram.html\n\n","x":-2778,"y":-1547,"width":450,"height":100,"color":"5"},
