		{"id":"a9436b501b0a8044","type":"text","text":"# Performance of Heat Geek HP in English home 2026\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CFSZViBzlIQ \n\nYoutube video on home replacement of gas boiler with heat pump. he did not change out the radiators and hot water tank, which some companies deemed too small for a heat pump. The whole install costed $2150 pounds with ~={red}heat geek's zero disrupt=~ installation plan. Installation completed in 2 days for a Vaillant HP.\n\t- It's very hard to quantify the heat quality change\n\t- There is a behavior change as they didn't need to adjust the heat pump based of usage needs (It was just on the time)\n\t- Did not need emergency heaters and the water tank upgrades and radiator upgrades were not necessary.\n\nHeat Geek has a performance guarantee for 5 years. They also do energy consultations for the proper design of heat pumps.\n- Vibration of wall mounted units was a problem. Can be prevented through ground mounted units.","x":-2282,"y":-1325,"width":362,"height":80,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"4d5fc6d1ccbd1ca5","type":"text","text":"# FPP-Reg-FCAS Recovery - To what extent has it succeeded and could it be improved 23.07.2026\n\nhttps://wattclarity.com.au/articles/2026/07/davescott-mpfr-and-fpp-part04-improvements/?_gl=1*1tyt754*_ga*MTUyMDYyOTAyMy4xNzgwNTI2NzEy*_ga_4ZTT9PF6TR*czE3ODUxMTUxMTIkbzEwJGcxJHQxNzg1MTE1MTIyJGo1MCRsMCRoMA..\n\n## Mandatory Primary Frequency Response\n\nMPFR was implemented across scheduled generating units and batteries to provide fast local response that keeps frequency in control.\n- This does not mean dispatch error has disappeared, and secondary control through regulation FCAS is still needed to correct error that persist.\n\n## FPP-Reg-FCAS\n\nThis aimed to quantify and price dispatch errors so that units that cause errors pay units that help correct them.\n- FPP identifies the good and bad deviation\n- Requirement for Corrective Response (RCR) calculates the corrective work required and converts that performance into settlement outcomes.\nThis mechanism uses the co-optimised Regulation FCAS market as an input and inherits imperfections in how the market is procured and use.\n- Co-optimised for price and frequency control\n\n## Implications\n\nParticipants consider the cost of dispatch error when pricing energy and FCAS when deciding whether unit control or generating at the margin is more profitable.\n- There is a large range in settlement indicates that participants can manage settle costs if it is profitable to do so. This varies based on\n\t- PPA LGC,\n\t- energy,\n\t- security constraint curtailment that affects the unit at the time\n\n## Proposition\n\nThis article suggests that \n- net error presented by non-scheduled load, \n- residual forecast error, and \n- semi-scheduled units can help inform future regulation FCAS requirements, as they drive FPP-reg-FCAS recovery settlement pattern\n\n![[Screenshot 2026-07-27 144309.png]]\n\nThere are two observations here in the time average data over a quarter\n- RCR increases in the middle of the day due to dispatch error from forecast based resources (solar)\n- Enabled FCAS (not the used), is static and does not change with demand\n\nThus, regulation FCAS can be adjusted more dynamically. Such as how they can be increased at certain times of the day or requirements can shift from lower reg to raise reg.\n\n## RCR is not a good candidate to drive regulation FCAS procurement because it is a gross dispatch error\n\nIts goal is to ensure sufficient settlements in FPP.\n\n- If system dispatch error are high and the usage of regulation FCAS is high, then there is demand for secondary control. The price of regulation FCAS will go up and units on the AFC-Regulation will capture greater FPP revenue and may reduce primary control response.\n- Conversely if errors are low, then units may switch to primary control response to capture more of that revenue stream\n\nThe frequency measure is also not a good candidate, because it requires the AGC to estimate the primary response of the system.\n\n## How can we dynamically size the FCAS Regulation Recovery as mentioned?\n\nRegulation FCAS can target the net dispatch error from semi-scheduled and residuals, derived from the inverse of the scheduled unit deviation. When the net dispatch error from semi-scheduled unit is driving FPP-Reg-FCAS recovery, then it should also drive the regulation FCAS outcome.\n- If dispatch error and regulation usage is both high, AEMO can procure more regulation FCAS, which will increas prices, Reg-FCAS recovery amounts and FPP settlements, raising the marginal price signal for dispatch error.\n\n![[Screenshot 2026-07-27 150857.png]]\n\nThere are roughly consistent errors between the frequency measure (RCR), the enabled units, and the used units. \n- The units considered good under MPFR would be assessed as good under the FPP-reg-FCAS recovery\n- But there are times when the periods in the FM seem to switch more readily than the net errors.\n\t- An explanation for this is that the dispatch error causing the high frequency is managed by units with positive error (which means that they are over generation. Thus by reducing their generation error, but remaining positive overall, means that the FM now classifies them as being bad)\n\t- These units that have positive generation have increased dispatch in response to the AGC. They may largely accommodate the need to reduce dispatch error by reducing generation as their AGC raise signal fall away to the scheduled trajectory, or is simply the trajectory catching up to their generation, or some limited primary control response. It may be the case that they are signaled by AGC to reduce dispatch, although the AGC has rules to prevent this.\n- This is also due to tight frequency deadbands\n\nThe article shows that raise regulations are mainly caused by semi-scheudled and non-scheduled generation falling below their dispatch targets. Regulation FCAS and FPP creates unnecessary settlements for the Lower direction.\n- Regulation FCAS having ex-ante enabled quantities that aren't used\n- FPP are wasted as it used FM that despite being smoothed, change signs frequently when errors are being accommodated by reducing a positive dispatch error on a scheduled unit.\n- Supported by the fact that total good error in the lower is the same scale as total good error in the raise. But this contradicts the fact that errors are placed on the system by semi-scheduled loads. Thus, why are we judging performance using FM when operating around a tight 15MHz deadband for FPP?\n\n","x":804,"y":-42,"width":494,"height":171,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"e6a65720d69668c9","type":"text","text":"# AGC Controls\n\nAGC uses a PI controller to estimate the MW imbalance for a given frequency deviation. The proportional component converts the frequency error into an immediate MW response. While the Integral component accumulates that response over time. The AGC then combines, smooths, and filters this signal through dead bands, range limits to allocate adjustments to Regulation FCAS-enabled units.\n\n## The problem\n\nWhen many generators and batteries are operating under the MPFR, frequency may be less sensitive to dispatch error due to inertia. Therefore, the AGC may underuse the Regulation FCAS units.\n- Returning the AGC to respond directly to the underlying dispatch error could allow AGC regulation FCAS to correct error faster, reduce primary response, and increase FPP settlements for FCAS-enabled units.\n- We can do this by relying more on 4s measurements to estimate system error (the maximum and minimum RCR and Suage in the 75 4s measurements of a dispatch window), rather than using the FM.","x":1554,"y":24,"width":426,"height":256,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"16f4698214309e4b","x":-1899,"y":-2359,"width":250,"height":72,"color":"5","type":"text","text":"# ~={cyan}Aluminium Production=~"},
		{"id":"bd0bad5c2421aebc","x":-1790,"y":-2737,"width":450,"height":177,"color":"5","type":"text","text":"# Circulating fluidised gas bed\n\nReplaces rotary kilns for the calcination process that occurs at $~1000\\degree C$.\n- Calcination is just heating solid materials to high temperatures without melting it to cause phase transitions or chemical decompositions.\n\n## Rotary kilns\n\nThey slowly rotate to facilitate uniform heating and material mixing\n\n## Fluidised bed calciner\n\nSuspend fine particles with an upward flow of heated gas, resulting in highly uniform temperature and reaction efficiency. Also effectively captures waste heat to the improve energy efficiency."},
		{"id":"43f199c6319db60e","x":-2036,"y":-1564,"width":376,"height":424,"type":"text","text":"# Material Vapour Recompression (MVR)\n\nCaptures the latent heat within vapour mixtures "}
	],
